ing the April surplus.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The resolution variant is the nominal, not-seasonally-adjusted first official print in MTS Table 1, monthly Deficit/Surplus (-), not fiscal-year-to-date totals, receipts, outlays, or later revised history.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence and July seasonal prior use the FY2024-FY2025 July sample plus the fetched FY2026 January-May flow history. Using the eight fetched FY2026 monthly signed flows [284.333, 173.277, 144.752, 94.615, 307.501, 164.100, -215.024, 292.648], sigma = 169.04. The 80% half-width is roughly 1.28*sigma = 216.37; centering the forecast at 285 gives unrounded bounds 68.63 and 501.37, rounded to 69 and 501.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Upside risk is a deficit above the interval from concentrated interest, defense, or benefit outlays and weaker receipts. Downside risk is a smaller deficit or surplus from unusually strong receipts; a deficit above $501 billion or a surplus below $69 billion would land outside the interval.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point estimate = 285 usd_billions; 80% interval = 285 +/- 216.37, rounded to [69, 501] usd_billions.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":285,\"ciLow\":69,\"ciHigh\":501}]}"}}
