1 percent, 2025-11 = 55.8 percent, 2026-01 = 58.3 percent, and 2026-02 = 56.0 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference class: the most relevant outside view is Alabama's own post-unwinding original first-print run from July 2025 through February 2026, centered around 55.2 percent across the five inspected points and with a latest value of 56.0 percent. I use Alabama persistence rather than a national average because this resolves one state row.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: the level is mid-to-high 50s; momentum from July 2025 to January 2026 was positive but February slipped to 56.0; one-off monthly cohort composition can move both completed-renewal and ex parte counts; the policy mechanism is continued CMS pressure for ex parte renewals but no known Alabama-specific automation shock in the checked context.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is latest-value persistence with a damped local trend, using the observed official-source-derived sample 2025-07, 2025-09, 2025-11, 2026-01, and 2026-02. The update adds +1.0 pp for the 2025-to-early-2026 improvement signal and +0.1 pp for CMS compliance pressure, partly offset by the February dip, moving 56.0 to 57.1. The 80% interval uses realized first-print swings in this Alabama sample, then widens for cohort mix and missing-month uncertainty.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: upside outside the interval would require a sustained automation or data-match improvement pushing Alabama above roughly 62 percent. Downside outside the interval would require a manual-heavy renewal cohort, data-source outage, or operational regression pushing the share below roughly 52 percent. A schedule counter-consideration is that CMS publishes this series monthly but I did not verify a CMS page explicitly naming 2026-12-15 for the August 2026 first print.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Observed changes in the inspected Alabama sample were +2.5, +1.7, +2.5, and -2.3 pp, with mean absolute change about 2.25 pp and max absolute change 2.5 pp. Point calculation: latest 56.0 plus damped trend and operational-compliance adjustment of +1.1 pp equals 57.1 percent. Interval calculation: widen the observed 2.25 pp typical move by about 2.3 times for the longer August horizon, missing months, and renewal-cohort mix, giving lower half-width 5.2 pp and upper half-width 5.1 pp, or 51.9 to 62.2 after rounding.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":57.1,\"ciLow\":51.9,\"ciHigh\":62.2}]}\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/manifest.json:4:  \"series\": \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\",\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/manifest.json:8:    \"catalogSlug\": \"medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-al\",\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/manifest.json:9:    \"dataPointId\": \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\",\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/manifest.json:12:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolves to Alabama's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it (expected roughly three to four months after the period). Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal.\",\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-57-39z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-al-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/manifest.json:14:    \"series\": \"cms.medicaid_pi.ex_parte_renewal_share.al.aug_2026\",\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-0