erived context at the 2026-06-27 run.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Base-rate/reference-class anchor: the most relevant outside view is Alaska's own post-unwinding first-print run from July 2025 through February 2026, centered in the mid-to-high 50s. I put more weight on the latest 56.2 percent and the January-February rebound than on a smooth national or multi-state average because this resolves a single Alaska state row.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Level, momentum, and mechanism: the recent level is below the September-November 2025 plateau near 61.5 percent but above the January 2026 dip. Momentum is mildly negative after the late-2025 fall, while operational policy pressure and data-match reuse argue for partial persistence rather than a collapse.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: prior model is Alaska latest-value persistence with a damped local trend, using the observed official-source subset 2025-07, 2025-09, 2025-11, 2026-01, and 2026-02 rather than a complete monthly panel. The -1.5 pp level update, -0.8 pp renewal-cohort volatility update, and -0.5 pp rebound-skepticism update are judgmental adjustments grounded in the 7.5 pp late-2025-to-January drop and partial February rebound, moving 56.2 to 53.4. The 80% interval starts from realized Alaska swings of roughly 2 to 8 pp and is widened to about +/-12.8 pp for small-denominator, missing-month, and system-release risk.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-consideration: upside outside the interval would require a durable system or data-match improvement that returns Alaska above the 2025-09 to 2025-11 plateau, roughly above 66 percent. Downside outside the interval would require a failed renewal batch, data-source outage, or unusually manual-heavy cohort that pushes the share near or below 40 percent.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Point calculation: start with latest 56.2 percent and apply a damped six-month trend plus operational-risk adjustment of -2.8 percentage points, giving 53.4 percent. Interval calculation: center 53.4, set a widened lower half-width of 12.9 pp and upper half-width of 12.8 pp from Alaska first-print swings and small-state denominator risk, yielding 40.5 to 66.2 after rounding.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Resolution-date note: the target ledger provides 2026-12-15 for this August 2026 CMS fixed-vintage row, while the official CMS public pages checked through the local run record establish the monthly data.Medicaid.gov release vehicle but did not expose a separate future dated placeholder for this exact August 2026 state row. I keep the forecast tied to the canonical ledger target and bind resolution to the first official CMS dataset print.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":53.4,\"ciLow\":40.5,\"ciHigh\":66.2}]}\n./records/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-59-59z-cms-medicaid-pi-ex-parte-renewal-share-ar-aug-2026-august-2026-reporting-period/raw_response.txt:1:{\"slug\":\"medicaid-ex-parte-share-aug-2026-ar\",\"country\":\"US\",\"type\":\"data\",\"title\":\"Arkansas Medicaid ex parte renewal share, Aug. 2026\",\"question\":\"CMS State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data, Arkansas state row, August 2026 reporting period, original first-print submission: ex parte renewals as a share of completed Medicaid renewals.\",\"unit\":\"percent\",\"pointEstimate\":89.8,\"ciLow\":85.6,\"ciHigh\":93.9,\"confidence\":0.8,\"resolutionDate\":\"2026-12-15\",\"resolutionSource\":\"CMS, State Medicaid and CHIP Eligibility Processing Data (data.medicaid.gov)\",\"resolutionSourceUrl\":\"https://data.medicaid.gov/dataset/5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c\",\"resolutionRule\":\"Resolves to Arkansas's ex parte renewal share for the August 2026 reporting period, computed from the original (O) Arkansas submission row in CMS dataset 5abea2e0-3f8e-4b49-a50d-d63d5fd9103c when CMS first publishes it. Numerator and denominator as published; share computed to one decimal percent. Ignore later updated submissions or