cent change, matching ABS one-decimal reporting.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:539:                \"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:546:                \"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:553:                \"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:560:                \"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:575:          text: \"The resolver is the first ABS print for Building Approvals, Australia, May 2026: seasonally adjusted total dwelling units approved, monthly percent change. The July 8 updated-information item is excluded because the target is first print.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:589:            \"Fetched April 2026 first-print context: total dwelling units approved fell 3.4% to 16,710; private sector houses fell 1.0% to 10,088; private sector dwellings excluding houses fell 3.6% to 6,403; trend total dwelling units were 17,363 with 0.0% monthly change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:619:          text: \"Mechanism split: detached houses were comparatively stable while multi-unit approvals drove the big 2026 swings. Policy pressure to lift housing supply is an upside force, but financing, construction costs, and project feasibility keep the near-term print noisy rather than persistently strong.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:627:          text: \"Counter-consideration and scenarios: downside outside the interval would be another large apartment-project drop or delayed approvals pushing the print below -10.0%; upside outside the interval would be a renewed multi-unit approval surge like February, above +16.0%; the central case is a partial rebound with no repeat of February's one-off jump.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:631:          text: \"Review disposition: accepted the critique to clarify the persistence/recent-volatility model prior, replace the unstable latest-release evidence URL with the stable April 2026 release URL, state the small-sample basis for the 80% interval, and clarify the unit as percentage points of month-over-month percent change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:761:          text: \"The resolver is the first ABS Labour Force, Australia May 2026 release, specifically the seasonally adjusted monthly change in employed people. The unit here is thousands of people, matching the ABS headline convention.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:1352:        \"Validated live Codex-backed thesis.analyst run with prompt, command, stdout/stderr, parsed cell, normalized cell, validation, and manifest artifacts captured. Prompt mode: fast. Value